{
    "schema_version": 1,
    "about": "Column-name terms that suggest a column holds MONEY, kept as data so the list is reviewable as a list and a diff shows exactly which term moved. Written for this package from the naming a Laravel schema actually uses; no third-party dictionary or linter catalog was copied. Two confidence levels, and the split is the whole point: a 'strong' term means money on its own, while a 'weak' term is ordinary English or German that happens to be used for money — 'value', 'rate' and 'wert' name a hundred non-monetary things, and a rule that fired on them would be noise nobody reads. A weak term therefore needs a second signal before anything is reported. Matching is on the lowercased column name, whole word or underscore-separated segment, never a substring: 'rating' is not 'rate' and 'evaluate' is not 'value'.",
    "matching": {
        "case": "the column name is lowercased before matching",
        "boundary": "a term matches a whole underscore-separated segment of the name, never a substring",
        "strong": "reports on its own",
        "weak": "reports only together with a second signal, which the rule defines and this artifact does not"
    },
